+Assume $\hc=G(n,q)$. We use the following observation. If a connected graph of order $N$ has degree $d<N/2-1$, it is neither a disjoint union of equal cliques nor its complement. If it is a generalised clique cover, the degree condition forces $Y=0$, in the notation of Definition~\ref{def:generalizedcover}. Connectedness then forces $s=1$, which gives $d\geq N/2-1$, a contradiction. If its complement is a generalised clique cover of $sK_t$, then the original graph contains an independent set of size $t$. Moreover, $t\geq N/2-d$: for $Y=0$ the complement degree is at most $2t-1$, and for $Y=1$ it is at most $N/2-1+t$.
+
+Write $L$ for the line corresponding to $\vc$ and $Q=(q^{n-1}-1)/(q-1)$. The first subconstituent is connected: for two lines meeting $L$ at different points, the line joining an off-$L$ point of the first to the intersection of the second with $L$ meets both. An independent set contains at most one line through any point of $L$, and so has order at most $q+1$. Writing $N_1=(q+1)(Q-1)$ and $d_1=Q+q^2-2$, we have
+\[
+\frac{N_1}{2}-d_1=\frac{q^{n-1}-q}{2}-q^2+1>q+1.
+\]
+Thus the observation excludes every possibility for the first subconstituent in Hypothesis~\ref{ass:classification}.
+
+The second subconstituent is also connected: for two lines disjoint from $L$, choose a point on each with linearly independent images in the vector-space quotient by $L$. The line they span meets both lines and is disjoint from $L$. Pairwise disjoint projective lines use disjoint sets of nonzero vectors, so an independent set has order at most $(q^n-1)/(q^2-1)$. Put $A=q^{n-3}$. The order and degree of the second subconstituent are
+\[
+N_2=\frac{q^4(qA-1)(A-1)}{(q^2-1)(q-1)},\qquad d_2=(q+1)(Q-q-2).
+\]
+The first formula counts the lines in the quotient, each with $q^4$ lifts. For the second use $\mu=(q+1)^2$, since a common neighbour of two disjoint lines joins a point of one to a point of the other. We have
+\[
+d_2+\frac{q^n-1}{q^2-1}<\frac{Aq^2(q^2+3q+1)}{q^2-1}\leq \frac{N_2}{2}.
+\]
+For the last inequality, it suffices that $q^2(qA-1)(A-1)\geq 2(q-1)A(q^2+3q+1)$. This holds for $q\geq3$ by $qA-1\geq(q-1)A$ and $A-1\geq q^2-1$, and for $q=2$, $n\geq6$ by $A\geq8$. For $(n,q)=(5,2)$, the needed inequality is $33+31/3<56$. Thus the observation excludes the second subconstituent as well, contradicting Hypothesis~\ref{ass:classification}.
 \end{proof}
