+Assume $\hc=H_q(2,m)$, and identify its vertices with $2\times m$ matrices, adjacent when their difference has rank one. At $0$, the first and second subconstituents consist of the matrices of rank one and two, respectively. Put $x=q^m$.
+
+Two rank-one matrices can be joined through a rank-one matrix with the column space of the first and row space of the second. Thus the first subconstituent is connected, and an independent set contains at most one matrix for each column space, hence at most $q+1$ matrices. Its order and degree are $N_1=(q+1)(x-1)$ and $d_1=x+(q-2)(q+1)$. If $q$ is even, $N_1$ is odd and $d_1<N_1/2-1$. If $q$ is odd, $N_1/2-d_1>q+1$ since $m\geq3$. Applying the observation in the proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:grassmann} excludes the first subconstituent.
+
+The second subconstituent is connected: for matrices with rows $(a,b)$ and $(c,d)$, choose a row $z$ outside the spans of $a$ and $c$, and change one row at a time through $(a,z)$ and $(c,z)$. An independent set contains at most one matrix per first row, hence at most $x$ matrices. The order and degree are $N_2=x^2-(q+1)x+q$ and $d_2=(q+1)(x-q-1)$. If $(m,q)\neq(3,2)$, then $x\geq3q+6$, which gives $N_2/2-d_2>x$. Thus the same observation excludes the second subconstituent.
+
+For $(m,q)=(3,2)$, the second subconstituent has order $42$ and degree $15$. The only remaining possibility is that its complement, of degree $26$, is a generalised clique cover. Substituting $st=21$ in the degree formula of Definition~\ref{def:generalizedcover} leaves $s=3$, $t=7$, $X=\overline M$, $Y=1$. Then $\hc_2$ contains $K_{7,14}$, contradicting $\mu=6$ in $H_2(2,3)$.
 \end{proof}
 
